  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe next round of heavy rain and strong storms have entered the \nnorthern part of the CWA. It will continue to sink southward \nthroughout the overnight hours into the morning. The environment is \nstill quite unstable and as these storms push south, they should \nmove into an area of stronger CAPE values. Effective wind shear is \naround 45 knots as of the most recent mesoanalysis from SPC where \nthe storms are located. Damaging winds is the main concern with the \nstrongest storms, but some small hail is possible as well.\nThe next round of convection will move in late this evening \ninto the overnight hours. A slight risk for severe weather is in\nplace for the northwestern part of our CWA from the SPC. \n\nAlong with the severe risk, we are also concerned for some flooding. \nThere is a flood watch out until 15z this morning. PWATs are around \n2-2.2 inches. We have been quite saturated as of late. Some areas \ncannot take much more without becoming inundated. Along and north of \nI-74, the FFG is much lower (6 hr: 2-2.5 inches, 3 hr: 1-2.5 inches) \nthan in the southern half of the CWA, since they have received much \nof the rain in the last day or so. The area around I-74 to Danville \nhas gotten ~2-5 inches of rain in the last 24 hours based off of \nMRMS estimates. WPC has put our northwestern counties in a moderate \nrisk ERO today.\n\nTemperatures remain hot as we make our way into the weekend. There \nis a heat advisory for Scott County to Clark County (and south) that \nis valid through Sunday. Further expansion north of the \nadvisory is possible as we go forward in time and get a better \ngrasp of how the next few rounds of MCS activity will evolve, \nboth spatially and temporally. Heat indices of 100-110 degrees \nare forecast through Sunday, but how far north these heat \nindices get will depend solely on the convection. \n\nThere is a marginal risk for severe weather again tomorrow area-wide \nand again on Sunday for areas south of a Schuyler to McLean County \nline with a similar environment in place as previous days. The \nforecast will continue to evolve as we get newer runs of the models. \n\nNext week may bring us a break in the active weather and the heat \nbriefly. Some guidance suggests a high pressure area setting up\nover the Upper Mississippi Valley / Great Lakes region by \nMonday, behind a cold front that is set to move through central \nIL during the day. Temperatures starting Monday into much of the\nnext week will be mainly in the upper 70s to 80s for highs and \n60s to low 70s for lows.",
